      "heading": "AWS - Interview Questions and Practical Answers",
      "explanation": "## Introduction\nAWS is a cloud platform. Instead of buying physical servers, companies rent computing, storage, networking and database services. A DevOps engineer uses AWS to build, deploy, secure and monitor applications.\n\n## Architecture\nA common production architecture uses Route 53 for DNS, an Application Load Balancer for incoming HTTP traffic, EC2 instances in private subnets, an Auto Scaling Group for capacity, RDS for database storage and S3 for files. CloudWatch collects metrics and logs. IAM controls access.\n\n## End-to-End Workflow\nDeveloper pushes code to Git. CI builds and tests the application. The pipeline creates an artifact or container image and stores it in a registry. CD deploys it to EC2 or EKS. The load balancer sends user traffic to healthy targets. CloudWatch monitors the application.\n\n## Interview Question\nHow would you deploy a highly available web application on AWS?\n\n## Detailed Answer\nCreate a VPC with public and private subnets in at least two Availability Zones. Place the load balancer in public subnets and application servers in private subnets. Use an Auto Scaling Group to replace unhealthy instances and increase capacity. Use Multi-AZ RDS when database availability is required. Store uploaded files in S3 instead of local server disks. Attach IAM roles instead of storing access keys on servers. Enable monitoring, encryption, backups and tested recovery.\n\n## Production Troubleshooting\nALB returns 502 or 503: check target health, application port, health-check path, security groups and application logs. EC2 cannot reach the internet: check private subnet route table, NAT Gateway, DNS and security rules. AccessDenied: verify the attached IAM role and policy. High cost: inspect idle instances, unattached disks, NAT traffic, snapshots and data transfer.",
