      "heading": "Linux - Interview Questions and Practical Answers",
      "explanation": "## Introduction\nLinux is an operating system widely used on cloud servers. DevOps engineers use Linux to run applications, manage services, configure networking, control permissions, inspect logs and automate tasks.\n\n## Architecture\nThe Linux kernel manages CPU, memory, processes, devices and networking. User applications run in user space. systemd manages services on many distributions. Filesystems store data, while logs record system and application activity.\n\n## End-to-End Workflow\nConnect securely to the server. Check system health. Deploy application files with correct ownership and permissions. Configure environment variables and service definitions. Start the service using systemd. Check listening ports and logs. Monitor disk, memory and CPU.\n\n## Interview Question\nA service stopped working after a deployment. How do you troubleshoot it?\n\n## Detailed Answer\nFirst identify the impact and the deployment time. Check systemctl status to understand the service state. Read journalctl and application logs to find the actual error. Check whether the process exists and whether the expected port is listening. Check disk space, inode usage, memory and file permissions. Validate the configuration before restarting. After the fix, confirm the service is healthy and monitor it.\n\n## Production Troubleshooting\nPermission denied: inspect owner, group and mode using ls -l and namei -l. Disk full: use df -h and du -sh to find large directories. Service fails: inspect journalctl and application logs. Port already in use: use ss -lntp to identify the process. High memory: inspect top, free and OOM messages in journalctl or dmesg.",
