      "heading": "Git - Interview Questions and Practical Answers",
      "explanation": "## Introduction\nGit is a version-control system. It records code changes so developers can work together, review history, create branches and safely return to earlier versions.\n\n## Architecture\nA Git repository contains commits, branches, tags and objects. The working tree contains files being edited. The staging area contains changes selected for the next commit. A remote repository such as GitHub stores shared history.\n\n## End-to-End Workflow\nCreate a feature branch. Edit files. Review changes with git diff. Stage selected files. Commit with a meaningful message. Push the branch. Open a pull request. Review, test and merge after approval.\n\n## Interview Question\nA wrong commit was pushed to the shared main branch. How do you undo it?\n\n## Detailed Answer\nFirst inspect the commit and understand what it changed. For shared history, use git revert because it creates a new commit that reverses the unwanted change. Run tests and push the revert commit. Avoid git reset on shared branches because it rewrites history and can affect other developers.\n\n## Production Troubleshooting\nMerge conflict: open the conflicted file, understand both changes, edit the final result, stage it and continue the merge. Accidentally committed a secret: remove it from the repository and rotate the exposed credential; deleting the latest file alone does not remove it from history. Wrong branch: inspect status and log before moving commits.",
