      "heading": "GitHub Actions - Interview Questions and Practical Answers",
      "explanation": "## Introduction\nGitHub Actions is a CI/CD automation service inside GitHub. It runs workflows when events happen, such as a push, pull request, release or manual trigger.\n\n## Architecture\nA workflow is a YAML file inside .github/workflows. Events start workflows. Jobs contain steps. Jobs run on GitHub-hosted or self-hosted runners. Artifacts store build outputs, while caches speed up repeated dependency downloads.\n\n## End-to-End Workflow\nA developer pushes code. A workflow checks out the repository, installs dependencies, runs lint and tests, builds the application, stores an artifact and deploys after required approvals.\n\n## Interview Question\nHow can GitHub Actions deploy to AWS without storing permanent AWS keys?\n\n## Detailed Answer\nConfigure AWS IAM OIDC federation with GitHub. GitHub provides a short-lived identity token. AWS validates the token against a trust policy restricted to the correct repository and branch or environment. The workflow assumes the role and receives temporary credentials. Give that role only the permissions needed for deployment.\n\n## Production Troubleshooting\nWorkflow does not start: check event filters, branch names and YAML syntax. Job is waiting: inspect runner availability and labels. Secret is unavailable: check repository/environment secret scope and approval rules. Deployment is denied: check OIDC trust conditions and IAM policy. Never print secrets in workflow logs.",
