      "heading": "Docker - Interview Questions and Practical Answers",
      "explanation": "## Introduction\nDocker packages an application and its required libraries into an image. A container is a running instance of that image. This helps an application behave consistently on a developer laptop, test server and production server.\n\n## Architecture\nA Docker image contains read-only layers. A container adds a writable runtime layer. The Docker daemon manages containers, networks, images and volumes. Registries store and distribute images.\n\n## End-to-End Workflow\nWrite a Dockerfile. Build the image. Run it locally and test it. Tag it with a version. Push it to a registry. Deploy the same image to the target environment. Use health checks, logs and resource limits.\n\n## Interview Question\nA container starts and then exits immediately. How do you find the reason?\n\n## Detailed Answer\nCheck docker ps -a and inspect the exit code. Read container logs. Inspect the configured entrypoint and command. Confirm required environment variables and mounted files exist. Check file permissions and dependency connectivity. A container normally stops when its main process exits, so the application must run in the foreground.\n\n## Production Troubleshooting\nContainer exits: inspect logs and PID 1 command. Cannot connect: inspect Docker network, published port and application bind address. Data disappears: use a volume for persistent data. Image is too large: use multi-stage builds and .dockerignore. Permission denied: check container user and mounted-directory ownership.",
