      "heading": "Kubernetes - Interview Questions and Practical Answers",
      "explanation": "## Introduction\nKubernetes manages containerized applications across a group of machines. It schedules containers, replaces failed Pods, provides service discovery and supports controlled application updates.\n\n## Architecture\nThe control plane includes the API server, scheduler, controller manager and cluster state store. Worker nodes run kubelet, a container runtime and networking components. Pods are the smallest deployable units.\n\n## End-to-End Workflow\nBuild and publish an image. Define a Deployment and Service in YAML. Apply the manifests. Kubernetes schedules Pods. Readiness checks decide when Pods receive traffic. Monitor rollout status and logs. Roll back if the release is unhealthy.\n\n## Interview Question\nUsers receive 503 errors even though the Deployment exists. What do you check?\n\n## Detailed Answer\nCheck the Ingress controller and its routing rules. Check whether the Service selector matches the Pod labels. Inspect Service endpoints to confirm ready Pods are registered. Verify container ports and readiness probes. Then inspect Pod events, logs, resource limits and NetworkPolicies.\n\n## Production Troubleshooting\nCrashLoopBackOff: inspect current and previous logs, command, configuration and dependencies. ImagePullBackOff: verify image name, tag and registry credentials. Pending Pod: inspect scheduler events, resources, taints, affinity and storage. Service has no endpoints: compare selectors and labels. OOMKilled: inspect memory usage and limits.",
