      "heading": "EKS - Interview Questions and Practical Answers",
      "explanation": "## Introduction\nAmazon EKS is AWS managed Kubernetes. AWS operates the Kubernetes control plane, while the customer manages worker capacity, networking, workloads, permissions and cluster operations.\n\n## Architecture\nAn EKS cluster has a managed control plane connected to worker nodes or Fargate workloads. The VPC CNI provides Pod networking. IAM controls AWS access, while Kubernetes RBAC controls Kubernetes API permissions. Add-ons provide essential cluster functions.\n\n## End-to-End Workflow\nCreate the VPC and subnets. Create the EKS cluster. Configure managed node groups or Fargate. Configure workload identity. Install required add-ons. Deploy applications using Kubernetes manifests or Helm. Monitor nodes, Pods, networking and control-plane activity.\n\n## Interview Question\nAn application Pod cannot access an AWS S3 bucket. How do you troubleshoot?\n\n## Detailed Answer\nFirst confirm which AWS identity the Pod is using. Configure IRSA or EKS Pod Identity for the workload instead of giving broad permissions to every node. Verify the service-account association, trust relationship and S3 policy. Check DNS, network egress and any endpoint policy. Test with the AWS CLI inside a safe diagnostic Pod.\n\n## Production Troubleshooting\nPod cannot obtain AWS credentials: check workload identity configuration and trust policy. Pods remain Pending: check node capacity, subnet IP availability and scheduling events. DNS failures: inspect CoreDNS and network access. Node NotReady: inspect kubelet, node conditions and CNI health.",
