      "heading": "Terraform - Interview Questions and Practical Answers",
      "explanation": "## Introduction\nTerraform is an Infrastructure as Code tool. Engineers describe the required infrastructure in configuration files. Terraform compares that configuration with its state and the real environment, then proposes changes.\n\n## Architecture\nTerraform configuration contains providers, resources, data sources, variables and outputs. State maps configuration resources to real infrastructure. A backend stores state. A locking mechanism prevents unsafe concurrent updates.\n\n## End-to-End Workflow\nWrite configuration. Run terraform fmt and validate. Initialize providers and backend. Review terraform plan. Apply an approved plan. Store state remotely and review changes through version control.\n\n## Interview Question\nTwo engineers try to apply changes to the same environment. How do you prevent state corruption?\n\n## Detailed Answer\nUse a remote backend with state locking and encryption. Restrict state access because it can contain sensitive values. Run production plans through CI and serialize applies. Require approval for destructive changes. If a lock remains, confirm the owner and whether an operation is still running before taking recovery action.\n\n## Production Troubleshooting\nUnexpected resource replacement: inspect plan and immutable fields. State lock error: verify lock owner before recovery. Drift: compare plan with actual infrastructure. Provider error: check credentials, region and provider version. Never commit state files or run state commands without understanding their impact.",
