      "heading": "Helm - Interview Questions and Practical Answers",
      "explanation": "## Introduction\nHelm is a package manager for Kubernetes. A Helm chart contains templates and default values that can be reused to deploy an application in different environments.\n\n## Architecture\nA chart contains Chart.yaml, values.yaml and templates. A release is an installed instance of a chart. Helm renders templates using values and sends the resulting Kubernetes resources to the cluster.\n\n## End-to-End Workflow\nCreate or select a chart. Set environment-specific values. Run lint and template checks. Review rendered YAML. Install or upgrade the release. Check rollout and application health. Use release history for controlled rollback.\n\n## Interview Question\nA Helm upgrade fails because a Kubernetes field is immutable. What do you do?\n\n## Detailed Answer\nRead the exact Kubernetes API error and inspect the rendered manifest. Identify which immutable field changed and why. Plan a supported migration or controlled replacement. Check data persistence, service availability and dependencies. Never delete a stateful resource blindly just to make Helm succeed.\n\n## Production Troubleshooting\nTemplate rendering error: inspect template syntax and values. Release pending: inspect hooks and Kubernetes events. Immutable field error: plan migration. Wrong environment values: verify the selected values file and command-line overrides. Rollback may not reverse external database changes.",
