      "explanation": "## Introduction\nNexus Repository Manager stores and distributes software artifacts. Teams use it to host internal releases and cache dependencies from external repositories.\n\n## Architecture\nHosted repositories store internal artifacts. Proxy repositories cache remote artifacts. Group repositories combine multiple repositories behind one URL. Blob stores hold artifact data. Users and roles control access.\n\n## End-to-End Workflow\nConfigure hosted and proxy repositories. Create a group endpoint. Configure Maven, npm or Docker clients to use Nexus. Publish versioned artifacts. Apply retention and cleanup policies. Back up repository metadata and blob data consistently.\n\n## Interview Question\nDevelopers cannot download dependencies from Nexus. How do you investigate?\n\n## Detailed Answer\nCheck whether Nexus is healthy. Inspect repository status and group membership/order. Verify the proxy remote is reachable. Check storage capacity, user privileges, TLS certificates, DNS and outbound network access. Use the HTTP status and Nexus logs to distinguish authentication, authorization, missing artifact and upstream failures.\n\n## Production Troubleshooting\n401: authentication is missing or invalid. 403: user lacks permission. 404: artifact or repository path may be wrong. Proxy failure: inspect remote availability and cached metadata. Storage full: inspect blob stores and cleanup policy before deleting anything.",
