      "heading": "Tomcat - Interview Questions and Practical Answers",
      "explanation": "## Introduction\nApache Tomcat is a Java web application server. It runs Java web applications, commonly packaged as WAR files, and handles incoming HTTP requests through configured connectors.\n\n## Architecture\nTomcat runs inside a Java Virtual Machine. Connectors accept network requests. Worker threads process requests. Applications run in web contexts. Access logs record requests, while Catalina and application logs capture runtime events.\n\n## End-to-End Workflow\nInstall a supported JDK and Tomcat version. Configure the service account and connectors. Deploy the versioned WAR file. Configure external environment settings. Start Tomcat. Verify the application URL, logs, thread usage and JVM health.\n\n## Interview Question\nTomcat becomes slow under load, but CPU usage remains low. What do you investigate?\n\n## Detailed Answer\nCheck request latency and access logs. Inspect busy and available connector threads, blocked threads, database connection pool waits, downstream service latency, garbage collection pauses, heap pressure and file descriptor limits. Capture thread dumps during the issue and correlate them with application and database metrics.\n\n## Production Troubleshooting\nHTTP 404: check context path and deployment. HTTP 500: inspect application stack trace. OutOfMemoryError: inspect heap, GC logs and memory usage. Thread exhaustion: inspect blocked requests and downstream waits. Port conflict: identify the process listening on the configured port.",
